<edge source="iran_gulf_conflict::capital_flow_to_equities" target="global_ai_power_shift::regulatory_risk" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">Surges in cross‑border equity flows into strategic AI firms during geopolitical stress—especially from state‑linked or opaque investors—trigger government investment‑screening and proposed restrictions, increasing perceived regulatory risk for the AI sector.</data>
  <data key="d38">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d39">iran_gulf_conflict</data>
  <data key="d40">global_ai_power_shift</data>
</edge>
<edge source="iran_gulf_conflict::capital_flow_to_equities" target="russia_ukraine_war::bond_yield" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">Net outflows from equities triggered by Iran–Gulf tensions shift investor demand into sovereign fixed income (flight-to-safety), increasing bond prices and lowering bond yields in markets financing the Russia–Ukraine war.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">iran_gulf_conflict</data>
  <data key="d40">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
<edge source="iran_gulf_conflict::capital_flow_to_equities" target="us_china_tech_rivalry::portfolio_reallocation" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">Conflict-related capital flight out of Gulf and nearby EM equities forces cross‑border downward adjustments in Asian equity allocations (including Chinese tech), producing measurable increases in net portfolio reallocation rates as managers rebalance to cash, US assets or commodity exposures.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">iran_gulf_conflict</data>
  <data key="d40">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
</edge>
