<edge source="iran_gulf_conflict::geopolitical_risk_level" target="global_ai_power_shift::oversight_intensity" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.8</data>
  <data key="d37">Escalation in Iran‑Gulf geopolitical risk prompts national governments to fast‑track export controls, security reviews and tighter oversight of AI research and firms to prevent perceived technology transfer and national‑security threats.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">iran_gulf_conflict</data>
  <data key="d40">global_ai_power_shift</data>
</edge>
<edge source="iran_gulf_conflict::geopolitical_risk_level" target="russia_ukraine_war::crossborder_capital_flow" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.65</data>
  <data key="d37">Heightened Iran–Gulf geopolitical risk drives global risk aversion and liquidity reallocation away from higher-risk, conflict-exposed jurisdictions, reducing net cross-border capital flows into markets affected by the Russia–Ukraine war.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">iran_gulf_conflict</data>
  <data key="d40">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
<edge source="iran_gulf_conflict::geopolitical_risk_level" target="us_china_tech_rivalry::geopolitical_tension" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">An escalation in Iran‑Gulf geopolitical risk prompts the US to heighten security postures, sanctions and supply‑chain safeguards that China treats as adverse moves, producing a concrete uptick in bilateral incident frequency and perceived US‑China geopolitical tension tied to tech and strategic supply chains.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">iran_gulf_conflict</data>
  <data key="d40">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
</edge>
<edge source="iran_gulf_conflict::geopolitical_risk_level" target="us_political_shift::policy_regulatory_expect" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.8</data>
  <data key="d37">A sustained rise in regional geopolitical risk from Iran-Gulf incidents leads US policymakers and regulators to announce or prepare sanctions, export controls, emergency energy measures and defense-related rule changes, raising market and stakeholder expectations of more active and stringent policy/regulatory intervention.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">iran_gulf_conflict</data>
  <data key="d40">us_political_shift</data>
</edge>
<edge source="iran_gulf_conflict::geopolitical_risk_level" target="us_political_shift::political_power_distribu" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">A sharp escalation of Iran-Gulf tensions increases national-security salience and public demand for decisive executive action, which tends to strengthen incumbents and expand executive discretion relative to the legislature, producing measurable shifts in political power distribution over the following election cycle.</data>
  <data key="d38">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d39">iran_gulf_conflict</data>
  <data key="d40">us_political_shift</data>
</edge>
