<edge source="us_china_tech_rivalry::geopolitical_tension" target="global_ai_power_shift::oversight_intensity" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.85</data>
  <data key="d37">A rise in geopolitical_tension (incidents, accusations, or escalation risk) causes governments to rapidly expand export controls, foreign‑investment reviews and interagency vetting of cross‑border AI collaborations, directly increasing regulatory and legislative scrutiny intensity.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d40">global_ai_power_shift</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_tech_rivalry::geopolitical_tension" target="iran_gulf_conflict::geopolitical_risk_level" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">An uptick in US–China tech rivalry prompts China to harden diplomatic and security ties with Iran (e.g., increased arms, intelligence or diplomatic backing) and reduces Chinese cooperation with U.S. de-escalation efforts, directly raising the probability of confrontations and incidents in the Gulf.</data>
  <data key="d38">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d40">iran_gulf_conflict</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_tech_rivalry::geopolitical_tension" target="iran_gulf_conflict::energy_commodity_price_l" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.65</data>
  <data key="d37">Heightened US–China tensions trigger increased naval deployments and precautionary shipping restrictions or insurance-cost spikes on routes serving the Gulf (due to higher incident risk), which raises risk premia and pushes up energy commodity prices.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d40">iran_gulf_conflict</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_tech_rivalry::geopolitical_tension" target="russia_ukraine_war::oil_supply_risk" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.55</data>
  <data key="d37">Escalation in US–China tech friction pushes Beijing and Moscow into deeper energy/security cooperation, leading Russia to reallocate exports toward Asian buyers and prompting route changes and supply‐prioritization that raise the probability of disruptive shortages or re-routing for European and global customers.</data>
  <data key="d38">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d40">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_tech_rivalry::geopolitical_tension" target="us_political_shift::political_power_distribu" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.8</data>
  <data key="d37">Acute rises in US–China tech friction (incidents, sanctions, or covert operations) prompt executive-branch requests for emergency authorities and congressional delegations of national-security decision-making, shifting de facto policymaking power toward the executive.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
